.TH CRON 1
.SH NAME
cron, crond \- run recurring commands and queued at jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cron
[\-f] [\-c file] [\-s spool] [\-\-no-at] [\-t | \-\-once [\-\-time epoch]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run in the foreground for svcd supervision. crond is a symbolic-link alias.
At startup, then once for each new epoch minute, reload /etc/crontab and
launch matching commands with /bin/sh -c. Jobs may overlap and output is
inherited from cron, normally the service log. Standard input is /dev/null
and the initial working directory is /. PATH defaults to /bin:/usr/bin if absent.
The local timezone follows TZ or /etc/TZ.
.PP
Each non-comment line contains minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of month
(1-31), month (1-12), day of week (0-7; 0 and 7 mean Sunday), then a command.
Fields accept *, comma lists, inclusive ranges and /step suffixes.
When both day fields are restricted, either may match; otherwise both match.
A field beginning with * has wildcard day semantics, including */step.
There is no username column. Commands run with cron's credentials.
.PP
Each minute also starts /bin/atrun for the selected queue, independently of
crontab errors. Missed minutes are not replayed. Backward wall-clock changes
pause dispatch until the previous epoch minute is exceeded; repeated local
DST times at distinct epoch minutes may run twice. Timezone changes are read
on the next minute. Malformed crontabs reject the entire recurring batch.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Remain in the foreground; this is always the behavior.
.TP
.B \-c file
Select another crontab. It must be a regular file owned by cron's effective
user, without group or other write permission. Symbolic links are rejected.
.TP
.B \-s spool
Select an absolute private at queue directory. Default: /var/spool/at.
.TP
.B \-\-no-at
Disable the queue runner, retaining recurring commands only.
.TP
.B \-t
Validate the crontab and exit without launching any job.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Dispatch the current minute once and wait for direct children.
Return failure if configuration, dispatch or a waited job fails.
.TP
.B \-\-time epoch
With --once, match recurring schedules against a specified Unix timestamp.
This does not change the system clock or atrun's real current time.
.SH SERVICE
Installing /etc/services/cron.ini does not start cron.
Explicitly add cron to enabled in /etc/boot/services.ini on a system with
svcd boot integration, or use svc start cron on an existing supervisor.
SIGTERM/SIGINT stop new dispatch. svcd signals the service process group,
including its active jobs. Directly stopping cron alone does not cancel jobs.
.SH LIMITS
A crontab line holds at most 2048 native characters, excluding newline.
Definitions are dynamically allocated; exec and available process/memory
limits apply. Environment assignments in crontab, @daily macros, mail delivery,
and special percent-to-stdin syntax are not implemented. Percent is literal
shell text. Edit /etc/crontab directly; a crontab management command is not included.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for a clean validation/one-shot or normal service stop; 1 for validation or
one-shot execution errors; 2 for invalid command-line options.
Daemon job/configuration errors are logged and retried at later minutes.
.SH SEE ALSO
at(1), atrun(1), svc(1)
